          "id": "harvest-wave",
          "title": "Harvest wave",
          "kind": "static",
          "unit": "",
          "description": "For each 100 m rice cell, the pair of radar passes in which its HV backscatter first fell sharply, from 29 September 2026 on; such a fall in this season is read as the harvest. The map misses much of the harvest: official figures and NASA's fire detections both point to more cut rice than it shows. So \"no harvest seen\" means that the radar saw no sharp fall, not that the rice was still standing. Cells that are not rice are grey.",
          "polarization": "HV",
          "method": "Harvest pair of each 100 m rice cell whose centre lies inside the site box, from HV: the first pair of consecutive passes, from 2026-09-29 to 2026-10-11 on, in which HV (mean of linear power over the cell, in dB) fell by more than 1.59 dB, the more negative of the 0.02 quantiles of the change on stable ground in any pair and on standing rice from 2026-09-17 to 2026-09-29. Rice: HH rose by at least 7.40 dB from the flood to the canopy. Warped to Web Mercator by nearest neighbour. Marker test, by a rule that the team's own record says was written down before any fire data was analysed: of the VIIRS fire detections with UTC dates from 2026-10-11 to 2026-11-14 that lie partly on rice (417 detections on 31 days, each weighted by the part of its 375 m square on rice cells), HH still called 68.4 % not harvested on the detection's date and HV 72.2 %; HV minus HH +3.8 percentage points, 95 % interval -5.7 to +10.9 (bootstrap over days). Decision: HV, by item 4 of the rule (the interval includes zero; HV gives a harvest pair to more rice cells by 4 November 2026: 31192 against 20503). Details: docs/METHODS.md.",
