      "story": [
        {
          "id": "two-dances",
          "title": "Punjab's fields dance twice a year",
          "text": "Wheat in winter, rice in the monsoon. The second dance ends in fire.",
          "layer": "hh",
          "date": "2025-11-21",
          "view": {
            "center": [
              74.93,
              31.21
            ],
            "zoom": 4.6
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "where",
          "title": "One place, a pass every 12 days",
          "text": "This is about 29 by 29 km, mostly farmland, in southern Tarn Taran, Punjab, and across the Sutlej river in Ferozepur district. NISAR passes over it every 12 days, at about a quarter to six in the morning. Its radar needs no daylight and sees through haze and cloud.",
          "layer": "hh",
          "date": "2025-11-21"
        },
        {
          "id": "reading-radar",
          "title": "How to read the picture",
          "text": "Brighter means more of the radar signal came back. Buildings are bright: the town of Patti is one of the pale patches. Calm water sends the signal away and looks black: the dark ribbons are rivers, the Sutlej among them, and the edge of the Harike wetland. The fields in between are the part that changes.",
          "layer": "hh",
          "date": "2025-11-21",
          "view": {
            "center": [
              74.9,
              31.23
            ],
            "zoom": 11.2
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "the-year",
          "title": "The year, pass by pass",
          "text": "Watch the fields. They brighten as the wheat grows from December to March, darken when it is cut in April, dip again when the paddies are flooded in June, and brighten strongly as the rice grows. We read these two rises as the two crops.",
          "layer": "hh",
          "date": "2026-09-29"
        },
        {
          "id": "june-flood",
          "title": "June, the fields become mirrors",
          "text": "Before rice is planted the fields are flooded. Smooth water reflects the radar away from the satellite, so a flooded paddy looks dark.",
          "layer": "hh",
          "date": "2026-06-25"
        },
        {
          "id": "harvest",
          "title": "October, the rice comes off",
          "text": "The same fields in HV, the part of the signal that leafy plants scatter. We expect it to rise as the rice fills in and to fall when the crop is cut. Watch for fields that turn dark from one pass to the next: we read that sharp fall as the harvest. In HV most of the rice does so between 23 October and 4 November.",
          "layer": "hv",
          "date": "2026-11-04"
        },
        {
          "id": "the-wave",
          "title": "The harvest wave",
          "text": "Each 100 m cell of rice is coloured by the pair of passes in which its HV first fell sharply; by 4 November that is about two thirds of the rice. Yellow means the radar saw no sharp fall, not that the rice still stood: officials reported more of the harvest done, and an optical check on Copernicus Sentinel-2 data suggests that about half to four fifths of the yellow cells had lost their green by then (three of its dates look hazy, and greenness alone cannot tell a cut field from a ripe one). The same check suggests the map's order is right: the later it dates a cell's harvest, the greener that cell still was in early October. Contains modified Copernicus Sentinel data 2026.",
          "layer": "harvest-wave"
        },
        {
          "id": "the-fires",
          "title": "Then the fires",
          "text": "Each dot is a 375 m square in which a VIIRS satellite instrument flagged at least one fire, between 15 September and 14 November 2026 (NASA FIRMS). At the peak of the burning, from 24 October to 4 November, fire detections were denser on rice the map calls harvested between 23 October and 4 November than on land that is not rice, but the map's harvest dates do not sort them. From 5 to 14 November the detection densities of the rice classes could not be told apart, so our guess that fires follow the newest harvest is not supported.",
          "layer": "harvest-wave",
          "overlays": [
            "fires"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "what-next",
          "title": "What NISAR could do next",
          "text": "These are ideas, not results. A harvest map made after every pass could show the teams that send out straw machines where the rice came off since the last pass. The same radar could follow the sowing of the wheat, test whether burned stubble looks different from unburned, and keep a crop calendar where monsoon clouds hide the fields from optical satellites.",
          "layer": "harvest-wave"
        },
        {
          "id": "explore",
          "title": "Now look for yourself",
          "text": "What this can show is limited: one look every 12 days, one year of passes and one rice harvest, a harvest map of 100 m cells that hold several fields, and an analysis done after the fires. Click any field to see its year as a curve.",
          "layer": "hh",
          "date": "2026-11-04"
        }
      ]
    }
